macOS: mirror Tib.FiberData into the %gs block so GetCurrentFiber() works.

On macOS the %gs base is the host thread block rather than the TEB, so
signal_init_process() mirrors individual TEB fields into it: Tib.Self,
ThreadLocalStoragePointer and Peb. Tib.FiberData is not mirrored.

GetCurrentFiber() is a compiler intrinsic that reads %gs:0x20 directly, so Wine
never sees the call and cannot correct the value. A game whose job system reads
it therefore gets a host value and passes it straight back to SwitchToFiber,
which dereferences it and faults.

MEASURED (M1 Pro, macOS 26.6.2, engine x64-crossover26.3-r4, 2026-09-09):

  * Inside Wine, gs:0x30 and gs:0x60 track the TEB and PEB correctly, while
    gs:0x20 returns a constant 0x8ff on every thread and does not follow writes
    to TEB->Tib.FiberData. Wine's own fiber bookkeeping is correct throughout;
    only the mirror is missing.
  * Marvel's Guardians of the Galaxy (Steam 1088850) renders its splash under
    D3DMetal and then faults on "Play Game" reading 0x907, which is 0x8ff + 8,
    the offset SwitchToFiber reads from the fiber it is handed.
  * Writing the mirror by hand from PE code makes GetCurrentFiber() return the
    real fiber, and fiber switching then works end to end, including
    SwitchToFiber(GetCurrentFiber()), the pattern that faults without it.
    Reproducer: private/notes/fibertest3.c in the Highball repo, no game needed.

COST, also measured. Offset 0x20 is not a free slot on macOS: in a plain
x86_64 process it holds the thread's pthread QoS class, reading 0x20ff for
QOS_CLASS_USER_INTERACTIVE, 0x4ff for UTILITY and 0x2ff for BACKGROUND. The
three slots Wine already mirrors into (0x30, 0x58, 0x60) read zero there, so
they are free and 0x20 is not. Windows puts Tib.FiberData at the offset macOS
uses for the QoS cache, and the ABI fixes both, so a correct GetCurrentFiber()
and an intact QoS cache cannot both be had on this platform. This patch takes
the fiber pointer and loses the QoS cache, which is a userspace value read by
libdispatch and pthread for scheduling hints. The loss is confined to threads
that call the fiber APIs, which is why the mirror is not also written at thread
start: doing that would clear the QoS slot on every thread for no gain, since
Tib.FiberData is NULL there anyway. Reproducer: private/notes/tsdslot.c.

The guard is __x86_64__ alone rather than __APPLE__. dlls/kernelbase is built
by the mingw cross-compiler for PE, where __APPLE__ is never defined, so an
__APPLE__ guard compiles the mirror out entirely and the patch silently does
nothing (measured: the first build of this patch produced a kernelbase.dll with
no %gs store in it). On hosts where %gs maps to the TEB the store writes the
same address as the C assignment above it, so leaving it unconditional is
correct there too.

NOT VERIFIED: that this patch makes Guardians run. It fixes the defect above,
which is measured, but whether that defect is the only thing stopping the game
is untested. Note also that CodeWeavers' own testers report this game running
on M1 Max and M1 Ultra with D3DMetal on CrossOver 24.0 and 25.0.1, built from
this same tree, which this patch does not explain.

UPSTREAM: not yet reported.

Keep the mirror in step in the three places kernelbase changes the field
(ConvertFiberToThread, ConvertThreadToFiberEx, SwitchToFiber).

---
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/thread.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/thread.c
@@ -1142,6 +1142,26 @@
     fiber->actctx.stack_ptr = &fiber->actctx.stack_space;
     init_fiber_context( fiber );
     return fiber;
+}
+
+
+/* On macOS the %gs base is the host thread block, not the TEB, so signal_init_process()
+ * mirrors individual TEB fields into it. Tib.FiberData was never mirrored, but
+ * GetCurrentFiber() is an intrinsic that reads %gs:0x20 directly and cannot be intercepted,
+ * so a game's job system reads a host value and hands it back to SwitchToFiber, which
+ * dereferences it (Marvel's Guardians of the Galaxy: read of 0x907, i.e. 0x8ff + 8).
+ * Keep the mirror in step wherever Tib.FiberData changes.
+ *
+ * On macOS this slot holds the thread's pthread QoS class, which is lost for threads that
+ * use fibers. Windows and macOS both fix the offset, so the two cannot coexist. Not guarded
+ * on __APPLE__: this file is built for PE by mingw, where that macro is never defined. Where
+ * %gs maps to the TEB the store writes the same address as the assignment above it. */
+static inline void set_teb_fiber_data( void *fiber )
+{
+    NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.FiberData = fiber;
+#ifdef __x86_64__
+    __asm__ volatile ("movq %0,%%gs:%c1" :: "r" (fiber), "n" (FIELD_OFFSET(TEB, Tib.FiberData)));
+#endif
 }
 
 
@@ -1155,7 +1175,7 @@
     if (fiber)
     {
         relocate_thread_actctx_stack( &NtCurrentTeb()->ActivationContextStack );
-        NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.FiberData = NULL;
+        set_teb_fiber_data( NULL );
         HeapFree( GetProcessHeap(), 0, fiber );
     }
     return TRUE;
@@ -1217,7 +1237,7 @@
     fiber->flags            = flags;
     fiber->fls_slots        = NtCurrentTeb()->FlsSlots;
     relocate_thread_actctx_stack( &fiber->actctx.stack_space );
-    NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.FiberData = fiber;
+    set_teb_fiber_data( fiber );
     return fiber;
 }
 
@@ -1267,7 +1287,7 @@
     /* stack_allocation and stack_base never change */
 
     /* FIXME: should save floating point context if requested in fiber->flags */
-    NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.FiberData     = new_fiber;
+    set_teb_fiber_data( new_fiber );
     NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.ExceptionList = new_fiber->except;
     NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.StackBase     = new_fiber->stack_base;
     NtCurrentTeb()->Tib.StackLimit    = new_fiber->stack_limit;
